From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:44:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 173/342] Remote Control preferences: a duplicate's changes go
 to the duplicate

_UpdateProfileList() emptied the list with BListView::RemoveItems(),
which leaves the selection's first and last index behind: after New,
Duplicate or Import, CurrentSelection() still named the profile that
was selected before, so the new profile was shown highlighted while
the window showed, and changed, the old one. The items are now removed
one by one with RemoveItem(), which keeps the selection right, and
deleted (they leaked).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/preferences/remotecontrol/RemoteWindow.cpp | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/preferences/remotecontrol/RemoteWindow.cpp b/src/preferences/remotecontrol/RemoteWindow.cpp
index 98ffc2367c..eec53a7f53 100644
--- a/src/preferences/remotecontrol/RemoteWindow.cpp
+++ b/src/preferences/remotecontrol/RemoteWindow.cpp
@@ -810,7 +810,11 @@ RemoteWindow::_CurrentButton() const
 void
 RemoteWindow::_UpdateProfileList(int32 select)
 {
-	fProfileList->RemoveItems(0, fProfileList->CountItems());
+	// one by one: BListView::RemoveItems() keeps the selection's first and
+	// last index, so CurrentSelection() went on naming the profile that
+	// was selected (a duplicate's changes went to its original)
+	while (BListItem* item = fProfileList->RemoveItem((int32)0))
+		delete item;
 	for (int32 i = 0; i < fProfiles.CountItems(); i++) {
 		const Profile* profile = fProfiles.ItemAt(i);
 		BString text = profile->Name();
